 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Graph-square-root generator for arXiv:2010.05733
     2	
     3	| Profile field | Value |
     4	|---|---|
     5	| Track | **A — structural hardness** |
     6	| Native domain | combinatorics |
     7	| Object regime | finite discrete |
     8	| Computational core | graph |
     9	| Certificate form | matrix certificate (ordered maximal-biclique cover) |
    10	| Intuition | decomposition: see the Boolean adjacency matrix as a union of maximal all-one rectangles |
    11	| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
    12	| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 4 Lemma 6 |
    13	
    14	This is representational coverage through a reduction that is central to the paper, not an unrestricted sample of all VC-k Root instances. The solver is handed the paper's graph gadget and its bipartite cross-edge matrix; the hard search is the Biclique Cover instance embedded by the authors.
    15	
    16	## Problem and trust model
    17	
    18	The source is Golovach, Lima, and Papadopoulos, [*Graph Square Roots of Small Distance from Degree One Graphs*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.05733). A solver receives a finite graph (G) in the exact form of Section 4's reduction and must give (k) inclusion-maximal bicliques covering every edge of a displayed bipartite graph (B). The certificate determines the neighborhoods of the gadget vertices (Z) in a root (H). `verify` checks the bicliques and maximality, constructs (H), checks the designated vertex cover, computes (H^2) with integer bitsets, and compares every adjacency bit with (G). It never reads the planted answer.
    19	
    20	Generation is inverse, not solution-driven: sample (k) equal-sized bicliques, form their Boolean union (B), enlarge each sampled biclique to a maximal one, and apply the forward construction in Lemma 6. The generator therefore knows a certificate without solving the generated instance.
    21	
    22	All deterministic G1–G9(c) gates pass. **The mandatory multi-vendor oracle loop is not complete:** OpenRouter rejected every bare, hinted, and placebo request with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` before any scored attempt. The genuine transcripts are retained; this result must not be presented as oracle-hardened until that external quota is restored and the three runs are repeated.
    23	
    24	## Why Track A
    25	
    26	Section 3 Theorem 2 and Corollary 2 give an FPT algorithm taking (2^{2^{O(k)}}n^{O(1)}) time. That is the easy regime to avoid: a small fixed (k) would make this a false Track-A claim. The named ladder grows (k) from 2 to 16. Section 4 Lemma 6 reduces Biclique Cover with parameter (k) to VC-(k+4) Root, and Theorem 5 rules out (2^{2^{o(k)}}n^{O(1)}) time under ETH.
    27	
    28	That theorem is worst-case, not a theorem about this random planted distribution. Distributional evidence comes from the gates: on eight hard-preset seeds, five construction-aware heuristics and an exact set-cover DPLL over *all* maximal bicliques found no certificate. DPLL exhausted 2,000,000 nodes each time (1.167 s mean, 2.573 s maximum on the recorded local run). This is evidence, not a proof that the planted distribution is asymptotically hard.
    29	
    30	## Worked demo
    31	
    32	This is `render(make_instance(seed=0, **DIFFICULTY["demo"]))` in full:
    33	
    34	```text
    35	VC-k graph square root via maximal bicliques
    36	
    37	All graphs here are finite, simple, undirected graphs.  The square H^2 of a
    38	graph H has the same vertices as H, and two distinct vertices are adjacent in
    39	H^2 exactly when their distance in H is at most two.
    40	
    41	The input graph G has the ordered vertex groups
    42	  X = [3, 8]
    43	  Y = [10, 6]
    44	  Z = [5, 0]
    45	and six further vertices
    46	  u=7, v=11, w=4,
    47	  u'=9, v'=1, w'=2.
    48	
    49	Within this statement, Xi means the i-th vertex in the displayed X list, and
    50	Yj means the j-th vertex in the displayed Y list (indices start at 0).
    51	The X-Y edges are the 1 entries of this 2-by-2 matrix; character j of row i
    52	is 1 exactly when XiYj is an edge:
    53	  X00: 01
    54	  X01: 10
    55	
    56	All remaining edges of G are defined as follows, with no other edges present:
    57	X union Z union {u} is a clique; X union {v} is a clique; {u,v,w} is a
    58	clique; Y union Z union {u'} is a clique; Y union {v'} is a clique; and
    59	{u',v',w'} is a clique.  (A clique contains every edge between distinct
    60	vertices in the named set.)
    61	
    62	A biclique is a pair (A,D) of nonempty subsets A of X and D of Y for which
    63	every possible A-D edge is present.  It is inclusion-maximal when no vertex of
    64	X can be added to A and no vertex of Y can be added to D while preserving that
    65	property.
    66	
    67	Find an ordered list C0,...,C1 of exactly 2 inclusion-maximal bicliques
    68	whose union covers every X-Y edge.  Repeated bicliques are allowed.  By the
    69	construction, Ci specifies the neighbors in X union Y of Zi in a square root H;
    70	the fixed root edges are uv, vw, u'v', v'w', all u-X edges, all u'-Y edges,
    71	and every edge inside Z.  Thus your list is a compact matrix certificate for a
    72	square root whose designated vertex cover is Z union {u,v,u',v'}.
    73	
    74	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as a JSON array of exactly
    75	2 pairs [x_bits,y_bits].  Each x_bits is a 2-character 0/1 string whose
    76	character i selects Xi; each y_bits is a 2-character 0/1 string whose
    77	character j selects Yj.  Order inside each bitstring is exactly the displayed
    78	order, both strings must select at least one vertex, and the outer order is the
    79	Z order.  Example of the required shape (not asserted to solve this instance):
    80	<answer>[["10","10"],["10","10"]]</answer>
    81	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    82	```
    83	
    84	The planted answer is `[["01","10"],["10","01"]]`. `verify` returns `(True, "ok")`. Dropping the second biclique returns `(False, "answer has too few bicliques: 1 < 2")`. A person can solve this demo by hand: its matrix is a two-edge matching, so its two maximal bicliques are the two singleton edges.
